#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4938a3-53fd-58ce-afd7-e6fffbb2ecc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb4938a3-53fd-58ce-afd7-e6fffbb2ecc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283377} :answer-field/id #uuid "060c9d21-6a0d-5d7e-94a7-05081910f7ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5b3023-e7db-5320-9e61-615c23760b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "343" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e5b3023-e7db-5320-9e61-615c23760b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "343" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283379} :answer-field/id #uuid "e561095d-3212-5ce8-9835-6efb015eafe3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ae8b309-4e89-5f9b-a264-5719d7c09b8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338755" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{10})=(-17.457\\,603…)^{∘}≈-{17.458}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{17.458}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={342.542}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={342.542}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/821713cb7b71b2b15da5ecdf90c0e836234d280fa3753c71615b570017773672.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{17.458}^{∘} \\\\  & ={197.458}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{197}^{∘},\\,x≈{343}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "867f460a-7eaf-596d-b11b-3b7d8f18a65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76\\,432" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283382} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8d5fa9-d0cb-5ef5-9b3f-78c696abb0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283383} {:answer/id #uuid "cde10f81-2f58-5b78-bbac-959808afa852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283384} {:answer/id #uuid "f73cfff2-ad6f-5818-8f85-b971ef213314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86\\,234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283385} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9171bf-a46a-54ca-b34f-3a263cd935e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cde10f81-2f58-5b78-bbac-959808afa852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283384} :answer-field/id #uuid "90034ffb-94dc-5ae6-89dc-79066a8561b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec3770df-c9fa-54e5-8986-bd47aa0f662d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77321" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{7})=(-16.601\\,5…)^{∘}≈-{16.602}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{16.602}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={343.398}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={343.398}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={16.602}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={16.602}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/c86fca87f7f46c0155be358f377550a4adebf1a2040c874d55c00473766745f5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{16.602}^{∘} \\\\  & ={196.602}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈343.398\\cdot 196.602=67\\,513\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abcc90e9-08d8-58d1-b412-77918e87da9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283389} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d17c22-642f-5b7f-8934-89d506d260c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283390} {:answer/id #uuid "90c2b9ae-3f7b-5c14-b297-5957e28c63c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283391} {:answer/id #uuid "4676dd71-ea32-58a3-a668-4af39c4da408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283392} {:answer/id #uuid "6b662a31-f775-51f9-8709-1d7cdbe1dfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abcc90e9-08d8-58d1-b412-77918e87da9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283389} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be90a64-8c35-5b79-a188-338ec464b0a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9af1aa9-2dd4-5e9d-b4ac-0af807dab309" :question/math-academy-id "q-77789" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289b3974-0d37-58d3-901e-67237a58d55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283396} {:answer/id #uuid "184ba303-6640-5150-899a-8c6c1f445b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283397} {:answer/id #uuid "423667f6-28e6-5f46-bd87-80892aa4daf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283398} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb78b2e-b451-564a-92df-7d7c1944a446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283399} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1a176a-3fc6-55da-9e64-a764adc24b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "289b3974-0d37-58d3-901e-67237a58d55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283396} :answer-field/id #uuid "b697866e-ea36-5bf6-917c-7de9fcdb12d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6e22fa6-c896-5275-b8a4-0291a04e6faf" :question/math-academy-id "q-77804" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-2$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})(-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d226115d-4080-5334-b6fa-7b23e12cb43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283403} {:answer/id #uuid "dafa3bb2-a495-539a-856d-6ea151b8cc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283404} {:answer/id #uuid "f2792716-c2b3-59ed-a8e1-3496d61091f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283405} {:answer/id #uuid "8393d2ea-31bb-5084-8fcd-3ff05a3b95ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283406} {:answer/id #uuid "68b9ae7f-e65b-5232-b110-ca8b999b6987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68b9ae7f-e65b-5232-b110-ca8b999b6987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283407} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade101b5-3b74-56ee-a69c-583110e716ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97b65183-2af9-5046-876f-bba35987095c" :question/math-academy-id "q-77812" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+30=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-12$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation II has no solutions."}]}
